<worksheet xml:id="sec-quiz-one-proportion-ci-att-2">
  <title>Quiz: One-Proportion Confidence Intervals Attempt 2</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>Name: <fillin characters="45"/></p>

    <assemblage>
      <p><em>Confidence interval formulas and R command</em></p>
      <p>
        <m>\widehat{p}=\dfrac{x}{n}</m>
        <m>\qquad</m>
        <m>SE=\sqrt{\dfrac{\widehat{p}(1-\widehat{p})}{n}}</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\alpha=1-C</m>
        <m>\qquad</m>
        <m>z^*=\texttt{qnorm(1-alpha/2)}</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>E=z^*\cdot SE</m>
        <m>\qquad</m>
        <m>\text{CI}=(\widehat{p}-E,\widehat{p}+E)</m>
      </p>
      <p>Here <m>C</m> is the confidence level written as a decimal.</p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      Use the one-proportion normal-approximation method and assume its
      conditions are satisfied. Show your work or R commands. Keep full
      precision in intermediate calculations and report interval endpoints
      as proportions rounded to four decimal places.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- Source: 1propCI.docx, Question 8; changed the one-sided lower
       confidence bound to a two-sided 95% confidence interval. -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-quiz-one-proportion-ci-att-2-antibodies" workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In a hypothetical veterinary study, a simple random sample of 280
        animals is selected without replacement from a population of 28,000
        animals vaccinated under the same protocol. At the scheduled
        follow-up, 252 of the sampled animals have the specified antibody
        response. Calculate a two-sided 95% confidence interval for the
        proportion of animals in this population that have the response.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <!-- Source: 1propCI.docx, Question 4; both reported sample percentages
       and the 82% confidence level are unchanged. -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-quiz-one-proportion-ci-att-2-medication-placebo" workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In clinical trials of Lipitor (atorvastatin), 271 subjects received
        a placebo and 6.65% had allergic reactions. Among 857 subjects who
        received 10 mg of Lipitor, 2.34% had allergic reactions. Calculate
        an 82% confidence interval for the population proportion with
        allergic reactions in each group. Do the intervals overlap, and
        what does their relative location suggest about the allergic-reaction
        rates in the two populations? Explain using your intervals.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
